00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:33.060
And there's no
reliability product.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.170
Reliability is just a result
of lots of hard work, lots

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:42.980
of planning, having really good
design, and good practices.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.440
You can't just go buy
the reliability button.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.830
This is actually pretty
different on public cloud

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:50.685
than it is on prem.

00:05:50.685 --> 00:05:52.560
There's actually a lot
of companies out there

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.170
that will just try to
sell you reliability

00:05:55.170 --> 00:06:00.486
for money with appliances
that you can buy on prem.

00:06:00.486 --> 00:06:02.610
But that's not really how
it works in public cloud.

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:04.920
So you need to change
your thinking a little bit

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.810
away from this scale
up, and we'll just

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.510
add four more redundant
power supplies

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.060
and this kind of thing.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:13.200
And think about how
we're going to scale out,

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.283
how we're going to anticipate
that nodes are going

00:06:15.283 --> 00:06:17.580
to go down, and we'll
be able to notice that

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.360
and recover automatically.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:20.860
OK.

00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:24.875
So we'll think about an
example application, of course.

00:06:24.875 --> 00:06:26.250
How can you do
this kind of thing

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.540
without any kind of example?

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:34.900
Imagine you're a Muppet,
and this is your app.

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:35.400
OK.

00:06:35.400 --> 00:06:37.590
Here's this architecture
just to lay it out.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.070
It's pretty simple.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:43.000
We have an API backend
that we're developing.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:48.120
We have some users that
are represented by the U.

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.170
We have a load balancer.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.000
That's the LB.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.520
Your API backend is the A.
This is kind of the thing

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.050
that you're coding.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.550
And then you have an
external service, which is--

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.180
I call that a Vision API.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.490
It could be something,
but you're calling to it,

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.720
and it's not under your control.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:08.370
I made the sizes of
the dots represent

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:12.240
the capacity of that node.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:14.340
So if its users kind
of think about-- it's

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.350
enough users to generate
100,000 queries per second.

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.870
And queries per second is a
terminology we use in Google.

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:23.807
It comes from our
days in search,

00:07:23.807 --> 00:07:25.140
where people were doing queries.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.630
So a lot of people say
requests per second.

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:32.130
We just use this term,
queries per second.

00:07:32.130 --> 00:07:35.460
So the size of the
nodes is capacity,

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:40.620
and the size of the edges is
traffic flowing along them.

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:45.900
So if the edge gets bigger
than the dot, we're in trouble.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:47.970
Think about that,
and if the edge gets

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:50.091
a lot smaller than the
dot, that means we're not

00:07:50.091 --> 00:07:51.090
serving to our capacity.

00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:52.600
And that's probably
a problem also.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:08:02.450
So number one cause of downtime
is you just get overloaded.

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:04.150
You have a success disaster.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.310
Imagine you launch a
really great video game,

00:08:06.310 --> 00:08:08.870
and people really
like it 50 times more

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:10.400
than you thought they would.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:12.470
This happens all
the time on cloud,

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:17.270
because we have really
great networking and scaling

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:19.200
all around your application.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.490
So where you may
be used to on prem

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:26.660
have a little drinking straw
firewall or load balancer

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.960
in front of you or little
drinking straw internet

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:34.159
connection, that will shield
you from all sorts of load

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:34.873
problems.

00:08:34.873 --> 00:08:37.039
You just kind of don't see
them, because the packets

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:40.400
get dropped outside, and
users are complaining.

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:43.770
But as an application
developer, you'd never know it.

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:45.620
On our cloud, it's
really different,

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:48.950
because the load balancer
is super happy to send you

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:50.720
millions of queries per second.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:52.460
It loves to do that.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:54.055
The firewall is distributed.

00:08:54.055 --> 00:08:56.430
As you probably know, if you've
read the Andromeda paper,

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:59.960
the firewall is distributed, and
it's actually on all the hosts.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:02.300
So it can take any
amount of traffic.

00:09:05.005 --> 00:09:07.380
And the network is really
well-connected to the internet,

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:09.350
so you really don't
get into a situation

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.270
where users can't
reach you, because you

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:13.410
don't have bandwidth.

00:09:13.410 --> 00:09:16.449
So here in this scenario,
I've made the users

00:09:16.449 --> 00:09:17.240
three times as big.

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:19.880
They went to 300,000 QPS.

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:23.660
Now your application
is getting overwhelmed.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.940
It can't keep up with demand.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:32.000
It can only actually make 10,000
QPS back to the Vision API.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:34.810
So all of a sudden, this becomes
your effective throughput,

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:38.030
as you can only
do 10,000 QPS back

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:41.330
to the users of useful work,
because you're sitting here

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:43.706
dying under all their load.

00:09:43.706 --> 00:09:44.830
So I hope you can see that.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:46.490
At the bottom of
the screen, you can

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:50.640
see the node that's selected
has a blue circle around it.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:54.840
And you can see the edges
coming out of there.

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:57.830
So what do you do about
an overload scenario?

00:09:57.830 --> 00:10:02.730
Do you call the ops person and
say, hey, add more servers?

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:04.200
That's going to
take a lot of time.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:05.850
There's no way.

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:09.010
So what you need to think about
is, I'm going to shed the load.

00:10:09.010 --> 00:10:11.580
I've got to get rid of
load that I can't possibly

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.870
serve anyway so that I can serve
a little more valuable work.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:18.960
So think about the types
of traffic you're getting.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.620
If there's a type that's not
as valuable as some other type,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.420
maybe like toss that
on the ground first.

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.930
Just very quickly
serve an error,

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:30.450
because you're trying to just
defend yourself at this point,

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.270
just trying to get
rid of enough work

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:36.810
that you can do some useful
work and not just die.

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:40.610
So one way to
think about this is

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:43.860
to just calculate the
capacity of your service

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:48.030
like using the
load test or math,

00:10:48.030 --> 00:10:55.410
and figure out, OK, if I can do
100,000 QPS at my application

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.780
server, then everything
over 100,000--

00:10:57.780 --> 00:10:59.220
I'm just going to
keep a counter--

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:00.630
and everything over
100,000, I'm just

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:02.379
going to return an
error just immediately.

00:11:02.379 --> 00:11:05.310
And it's going to cost
virtually nothing.

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:08.370
Then at least you're
serving some useful work.

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.470
So it's a way to
artificially increase

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:13.650
the capacity of your
service, and you can actually

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:15.990
serve a nice error
to a user that

00:11:15.990 --> 00:11:21.990
tells them something better
than just like TCP timeout.

00:11:21.990 --> 00:11:23.080
It's pretty good.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:25.680
So the problem
here is that we're

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:27.790
serving nice errors to our
user, and we're saying,

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:29.880
yeah, our site's
having a problem,

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:33.630
and there's like a little
sad Tweety Bird or something.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:38.640
But we're still only getting
100,000 QPS of useful work out

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:40.890
of the Vision API.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:45.720
So it's not great, but we're
still serving some traffic.

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:47.610
And it's not the
end of the world.

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:52.260
So that brings us
to our next cause

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:56.790
of downtime, which is that
you get a noisy neighbor.

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:58.890
You did all this
nice work to compute

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:02.900
that your service can handle
100,000 QPS at the application

00:12:02.900 --> 00:12:03.510
layer.

00:12:03.510 --> 00:12:06.210
But you forgot about
the fact that you're

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:10.026
going to be running a backup
script on that server.

00:12:10.026 --> 00:12:12.150
And that's going to take
up a lot of-- that's going

00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:14.250
to take up a bunch of cycles.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:17.730
Or you have users, and as
soon as you have users,

00:12:17.730 --> 00:12:18.769
you get spam.

00:12:18.769 --> 00:12:20.310
And you're going to
get spammy users,

00:12:20.310 --> 00:12:25.380
and I guarantee you'll be
shocked at the size of the spam

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:28.920
compared to the size
of the real users.

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:30.990
I mean, it's very
easy for the spam

00:12:30.990 --> 00:12:37.140
to go 10x the normal volume
of the actual human users.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:40.180
So you really need to protect
yourself against this stuff.

00:12:40.180 --> 00:12:44.455
So if we just shed load
evenly just AB, AB,

00:12:44.455 --> 00:12:46.080
and we're just going
to decide to shed,

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.990
then we're going to actually
impact our real users

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:51.420
a lot more than we need to,
because we can actually be

00:12:51.420 --> 00:12:53.080
a little bit smarter about it.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:57.390
And we can decide which
queries to throw away.

00:12:57.390 --> 00:13:00.240
So the way to think about
dealing with noisy neighbors

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:04.710
is you put in place limits,
like you say, artificially

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:08.370
limit each user ID is going
to get 100 queries per second.

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:12.780
I can't imagine a human user
would do more than 100 queries

00:13:12.780 --> 00:13:14.020
per second.

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:18.330
And that really helps, because
a lot of the spambots, bad guys,

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:21.450
will be sending 100,000
queries per second.

00:13:21.450 --> 00:13:24.890
And that's really more
than is reasonable.

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:27.750
It's easy to detect.

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:30.390
Some people try to
do stuff like do it

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:33.600
by IP address or something.

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:35.700
But you have to check
your assumptions

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.040
a little bit about
how distributed

00:13:38.040 --> 00:13:39.552
the load is across IP space.

00:13:39.552 --> 00:13:42.010
If you have something like a
cookie that you're giving out,

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:44.850
then you know a little bit
more about the distribution

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:46.080
of the keyspace.

00:13:46.080 --> 00:13:49.110
So using user ID,
using a cookie ID,

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:51.660
using some hash of a
bunch of information

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:54.150
that you can get
from the HTTP request

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.570
is a pretty useful
way to keep counters

00:13:57.570 --> 00:14:00.804
on how many queries
people are allowed to do,

00:14:00.804 --> 00:14:02.970
and then cut them off when
they hit their limit just

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.230
to protect others.

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:09.580
So it's also good to
do this in combination

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:11.330
with a lot of different
similar strategies

00:14:11.330 --> 00:14:15.440
like limit the cookie to
100 queries per second,

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:17.870
and then maybe limit
each IP address

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:19.920
to 100,000 queries per second.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:22.980
And then maybe limit each--

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:26.570
I don't know-- user agent
to 100,000 or something.

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:29.480
And then you can kind of
build a layered situation,

00:14:29.480 --> 00:14:35.060
where it's likely that you'll
take out a bad guy before they

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:38.170
get all the way through.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:42.430
But inevitably, you're going
to take out some good guys too.

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:44.320
You'll still be serving
90% of the good guys,

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:45.767
so that's good news.

00:14:45.767 --> 00:14:47.600
But somebody will
complain, and they'll say,

00:14:47.600 --> 00:14:50.710
hey, you took down my IP
address, and I'm really mad.

00:14:50.710 --> 00:14:52.120
Why can't I access your service?

00:14:52.120 --> 00:14:55.240
So make sure that you make
these things configurable,

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:58.090
and don't just like hardcode
it into your code, where you'd

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:02.780
have to deploy new code in
order to restore service

00:15:02.780 --> 00:15:03.666
to your users.

00:15:03.666 --> 00:15:06.040
Make it configurable, so you
can just push up a new file,

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:07.630
and let them back in.

00:15:07.630 --> 00:15:10.385
Most users will say,
yeah, I'm bummed,

00:15:10.385 --> 00:15:12.760
but you said you were sorry,
and you fixed it right away,

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:13.900
so thank you so much.

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:15.441
They don't really
like it if you just

00:15:15.441 --> 00:15:19.450
say, I have to leave you out
of service for a week because

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:21.250
of technical issues
on my side, because I

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:24.820
have to burn a new release.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.510
They don't care.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:31.030
And you also can just alert
again to your ops team,

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:37.030
and whack the mole later
by pushing a firewall rule

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:41.170
or something up to a higher
layer, so it's cheaper to serve

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:42.977
or cheaper to reject
the bad traffic.

00:15:42.977 --> 00:15:45.310
First things first, though,
is just protect the service,

00:15:45.310 --> 00:15:47.790
protect the good
users, and keep going.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:53.490
So what happens when you
start rejecting people?

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:55.020
Well, they retry,
because they don't

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:56.670
know why they're rejected.

00:15:56.670 --> 00:15:59.790
So retry spikes are
a pretty famous way

00:15:59.790 --> 00:16:01.500
to generate a lot of load.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:04.770
And when you're dealing with
a micro service architecture,

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.500
it's pretty different,
because each layer

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:09.060
is retrying to the next layer.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:13.020
So you can get this compounding
effect pretty quickly.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:18.420
I've just illustrated here
300,000 QPS provisioned

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:23.790
at the API server, and the
users turns into 2.7 million

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:26.100
after it gets compounded
three times by the client.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:28.260
Then the load balancer
is retrying again,

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.780
and you can imagine what happens
if you put caching layers

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.080
and stuff in between here.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:35.070
It can get out of
hand really fast.

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.200
So watch out for
your retry spikes,

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:41.880
because this is the most common
cause of a cascading failure.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:46.290
Some little part of it goes
down, and then everybody piles

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.570
on and tries to get more,
really crushes that thing.

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:53.880
And they move over to the
next zone or region, whatever

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:56.669
your deployment unit
is, crush that thing,

00:16:56.669 --> 00:16:58.460
and nobody can get back
up, because there's

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:03.140
just so much noise
from all the retries.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:04.849
So what do you do about retries?

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:07.670
Well, this is about the
client and the server

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:11.290
actually being
polite to each other.

00:17:11.290 --> 00:17:13.210
A lot of it has to
do with the client.

00:17:13.210 --> 00:17:15.849
If you can control the
client, you're very lucky.

00:17:15.849 --> 00:17:18.069
Usually, this is a mobile
application scenario.

00:17:18.069 --> 00:17:20.170
You can control the
client a little bit.

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:23.500
There's a lot of time-honored
techniques of doing backoff,

00:17:23.500 --> 00:17:28.260
and we did a CRE
blog about backoff.

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:32.697
The classic is like exponential
backoff with [? jitter ?].

00:17:32.697 --> 00:17:34.780
There's kind of no reason
that exponential backoff

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:37.107
is the right amount.

00:17:37.107 --> 00:17:39.190
There's no reason you
should wait one second, then

00:17:39.190 --> 00:17:40.898
wait two seconds, then
wait four seconds.

00:17:40.898 --> 00:17:43.390
Maybe wait one second, and
then you wait 10 seconds,

00:17:43.390 --> 00:17:45.340
and then you wait a day.

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:47.800
It really depends
on your application.

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:50.509
Even with an exponential
backoff like of waiting

00:17:50.509 --> 00:17:52.300
like one second, two
seconds, four seconds,

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:57.360
you're still getting three
retries within a few seconds.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:18:00.100
It's unlikely that you've
actually fixed your service

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:01.060
in those few seconds.

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:02.800
So think about
having your backoff

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:05.290
in terms of the time that
you expect your service

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:08.020
to come back online.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:10.180
There's also
signals you can send

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:11.840
to the client from the server.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:17.710
You can say, hey, please try
again in an hour or tomorrow

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:19.000
or something like that.

00:18:19.000 --> 00:18:24.850
There's an HTTP header called
Retry-After that nobody uses,

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:27.040
but you can.

00:18:27.040 --> 00:18:31.510
When it becomes available in
browsers, then it'll work.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.130
Another really
powerful technique

00:18:33.130 --> 00:18:37.630
is to implement a retry
marker from the client.

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:40.992
So the client says, hey,
this is my first retry.

00:18:40.992 --> 00:18:42.200
Hey, this is my second retry.

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:44.810
Put it in a URL parameter.

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:47.960
So then the server can
see it, and they can say,

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:50.900
OK, I'm going to decide
how to shed my load,

00:18:50.900 --> 00:18:53.480
and I'm going to rank it by the
number of retries you've had.

00:18:53.480 --> 00:18:55.514
So if you're on
your fifth retry,

00:18:55.514 --> 00:18:57.180
probably you've already
given up anyway.

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:58.610
So I'll throw those away.

00:18:58.610 --> 00:19:00.526
And then I'll throw away
all the four retries.

00:19:00.526 --> 00:19:02.750
And then I'll throw
away all the thirds.

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:06.530
But I'll be really careful
to serve all the first ones

00:19:06.530 --> 00:19:07.310
that I see.

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:12.480
So that's a pretty
powerful mechanism.

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:15.980
Another mechanism that's
useful as a last resort is

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:18.530
tar pitting, where what
you do in the server is you

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:22.920
just wait, and you introduce
some artificial latency

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:25.370
so that the clients
can't retry very fast.

00:19:25.370 --> 00:19:29.360
So you basically try to use
up as much of their timeout

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:33.370
as you can get away with,
and then return to them.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:34.870
Even if you're
returning an error,

00:19:34.870 --> 00:19:36.720
you just wait, and then return.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:40.640
So you're kind of stringing
them along a little bit.

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:43.240
But it's something you can
do purely on the server side

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:46.517
to slow down the
request velocity.

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:56.350
So we were going right along,
and we're serving nicely

00:19:56.350 --> 00:20:00.060
until our Vision API, which
we thought could do 100,000

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:04.850
QPS, all of a sudden started
doing like 10,000 QPS,

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:06.890
because it just got really slow.

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:09.830
And we don't know why.

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:14.150
So now requests are piling
up at our API server,

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:16.100
and now we're
[? ooming ?], and now we're

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:18.394
not serving any traffic
back to the users.

00:20:18.394 --> 00:20:19.310
This is just terrible.

00:20:22.070 --> 00:20:25.110
The problem here really
is that the input

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:27.770
and the output of
your service are kind

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:29.990
of clueless about each other.

00:20:29.990 --> 00:20:35.240
So the solution is to
make the logic that's

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:38.780
accepting incoming requests
be aware of its ability

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:42.110
to service those requests
via the external dependencies

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:43.490
on the backend.

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:48.205
So if I can only do 10,000
QPS of the Vision server,

00:20:48.205 --> 00:20:49.580
I should really
only be accepting

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:52.680
10,000 QPS on the front end.

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:58.200
So the original load
shedding technique

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:00.570
that I suggested
of just deciding

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:02.160
that you could
serve 100,000 QPS,

00:21:02.160 --> 00:21:05.970
and then just throwing away
everything after that, Adrian

00:21:05.970 --> 00:21:07.980
Hilton, who's one
of our CREs, he

00:21:07.980 --> 00:21:11.130
called that Procrustean
load shedding.

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:14.670
Procrustes was one
of Poseidon's sons,

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:21.420
who ran kind of a gloomy B and
B, where he had an iron bed.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:23.712
And people would come, and
he would welcome them in.

00:21:23.712 --> 00:21:25.170
He would say, oh,
I have a bed that

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:26.820
will fit you just perfectly.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:29.750
And then if the poor sap was too
short, he would stretch them.

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:32.710
And if the poor sap was too
long, he would crush them.

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:37.440
And so it became a term that's
used for taking the problem

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:39.820
and fitting it to
a known constraint,

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:41.910
even if it doesn't
make any sense at all.

00:21:41.910 --> 00:21:47.250
So luckily, Theseus came along
and dispatched him one day.

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:49.350
So we don't have
to worry about him.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:54.780
But we still can't also rely
on that as a load shedding

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:58.020
technique, because when your
capacity kind of approaches

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:01.320
zero, then your assumption
that you could serve 100,000

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:04.050
QPS is wrong, and you
fall on your face.

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:10.170
So we need to think about being
really defensive in our client.

00:22:10.170 --> 00:22:13.050
And your client needs to
become like a defensive driver.

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:15.000
Like leave a lot of space.

00:22:15.000 --> 00:22:16.920
Make sure that you're
not going to overwhelm

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:21.600
your backend dependency,
and tell the upstream when

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:23.130
it's time to back off.

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:25.560
So you need to
think about what are

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:30.420
the timeouts that my clients are
making to me or having to me.

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:33.390
Like if they're going to
time out after one second,

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:36.030
don't set a three-second
timeout on your backend,

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:38.040
because by the time
you get an answer back,

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:39.780
your client's already gone away.

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:43.895
So you can work this through
in just your mental model

00:22:43.895 --> 00:22:45.270
of how the system
works, and it's

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:50.220
very much cheaper than
figuring it out in real life.

00:22:50.220 --> 00:22:51.870
And it's also
really nice to just

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:54.420
be able to just abort
your backend connection

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:59.940
after a second very cheaply if
the client's already gone away.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:04.270
But how do you know when your
dependency's going to go away?

00:23:04.270 --> 00:23:07.960
A lot of the dependencies
operate really,

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:11.100
really well most of the time.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:14.340
So cloud storage
is a great example.

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:18.930
It often has tens of
milliseconds latency,

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:23.790
and it's four nines
for many weeks.

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:27.780
And then by design, someone
does some maintenance,

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:31.470
and it goes up to
80-millisecond latency and three

00:23:31.470 --> 00:23:36.480
nines, which is like the
published SLA for it.

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:38.790
But if you've been
just relying on it

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:41.610
to work kind of how well it's
been working in the past,

00:23:41.610 --> 00:23:43.620
then you'll be very surprised.

00:23:43.620 --> 00:23:47.250
So what you want
to do with that is

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:49.050
do a disaster test,
where you simulate

00:23:49.050 --> 00:23:52.320
the worst possible expected
behavior of the service.

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:55.770
So you can put in
logic in your client

00:23:55.770 --> 00:23:57.930
to your backend that
artificially slows it

00:23:57.930 --> 00:24:02.170
down and makes it look
like the backend is bad.

00:24:02.170 --> 00:24:04.410
And then you can see
what your clients are

00:24:04.410 --> 00:24:06.311
going to do to react to that.

00:24:06.311 --> 00:24:07.310
So that's one technique.

00:24:07.310 --> 00:24:11.750
And of course, just
doing good load tests

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:14.990
will often tease a
lot of this stuff out,

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:20.766
especially doing load tests
that are very sudden and spiky.

00:24:20.766 --> 00:24:22.640
I mean, I know a lot of
people do load tests,

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:26.870
where they kind of
gradually ramp up the load.

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:29.120
And that's great, because
it's a very safe way

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:32.510
to do load tests that's unlikely
to come to find a problem.

00:24:32.510 --> 00:24:34.310
But the purpose of
the load test is

00:24:34.310 --> 00:24:37.730
to find the problem, so simulate
the scenario that you would

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:40.730
have if you had a zone
failover or something,

00:24:40.730 --> 00:24:44.510
where you all of a sudden get
a huge instantaneous load.

00:24:44.510 --> 00:24:46.660
That's the most fun
type of load test to do.

00:24:46.660 --> 00:24:48.650
And you'll really
find the problems

00:24:48.650 --> 00:24:51.840
before they occur in production.

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:54.770
The best possible
scenario to implement

00:24:54.770 --> 00:24:59.750
is like a dynamic load
shedding, where you can actually

00:24:59.750 --> 00:25:02.270
decide how much load to
shed on the front end

00:25:02.270 --> 00:25:04.250
based on how your
back end is performing

00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:07.490
and have some equilibrium
heuristic in your service

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:09.770
that can just
adapt all the time.

00:25:09.770 --> 00:25:12.680
That way, you can kind of
get the best performance

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:15.430
and the best availability
all the time.

00:25:15.430 --> 00:25:17.180
And one thing that's
kind of close to that

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:18.740
is being able to
at least manually

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:20.910
adjust your client parameters.

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:24.260
I know a lot of people just
put in retry three times

00:25:24.260 --> 00:25:25.550
with a one-second timeout.

00:25:25.550 --> 00:25:28.000
Just make it so that
you can configure those.

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:30.029
That can really
save your bacon when

00:25:30.029 --> 00:25:31.820
your client is being
too aggressive to just

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:34.014
be able to push a
config very quickly.

00:25:34.014 --> 00:25:35.930
Within a few minutes,
you can have your client

00:25:35.930 --> 00:25:37.320
back off really nicely.

00:25:41.290 --> 00:25:44.710
So all of that was in
the scenario of you

00:25:44.710 --> 00:25:48.529
have one VM serving
your service.

00:25:48.529 --> 00:25:50.320
And all of those things
are terrible things

00:25:50.320 --> 00:25:53.290
that can happen to
your service no matter

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:57.910
how big it is when you
have 300,000 QPS of demand

00:25:57.910 --> 00:26:00.370
and 100,000 QPS of
ability to serve it.

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:06.230
So let's say we actually want
to serve that 300,000 QPS.

00:26:06.230 --> 00:26:08.930
The bottleneck here is
between our application

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:11.030
and this external API.

00:26:11.030 --> 00:26:11.810
So what do we do?

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:13.050
We get a bigger VM, right?

00:26:16.630 --> 00:26:19.450
That's not a very
good answer, actually.

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:21.220
So the cloud is really
built to scale out.

00:26:21.220 --> 00:26:25.240
The thing that's different
with the public cloud from your

00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:28.720
on prem is that we have
a really good network,

00:26:28.720 --> 00:26:30.580
so adding new nodes
to the network

00:26:30.580 --> 00:26:33.940
doesn't really cost
anything in network space.

00:26:33.940 --> 00:26:36.880
You don't have like
this VLAN problem.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:40.130
And provisioning new
instances is super cheap.

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:42.850
So take advantage
of that, and use it.

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:48.010
A lot of people have
deep, horrible scars

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:50.830
about asking for a
new machine, and then

00:26:50.830 --> 00:26:52.660
having it take
months to provision.

00:26:52.660 --> 00:26:54.770
And you need to erase
that from your mind,

00:26:54.770 --> 00:26:56.770
and this is a beautiful
new world, where you can

00:26:56.770 --> 00:26:58.490
have a new machine in seconds.

00:26:58.490 --> 00:27:02.380
So make sure that your
application actually calls

00:27:02.380 --> 00:27:03.970
the APIs and makes
that happen when

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:05.920
its necessary,
because it's not going

00:27:05.920 --> 00:27:09.820
to be the last time that you
scale up or maybe scale down.

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:12.220
So you need to think
about how are we

00:27:12.220 --> 00:27:15.627
going to scale past the
boundaries of the components

00:27:15.627 --> 00:27:16.210
of our system.

00:27:16.210 --> 00:27:19.610
So VM is one.

00:27:19.610 --> 00:27:24.280
Like a Cloud SQL instance is
another pretty obvious one.

00:27:24.280 --> 00:27:28.090
Even like a GKE cluster
will have a size

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:30.990
that it can't go beyond a
certain number of nodes.

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:34.330
And there's some strange
scaling boundaries too.

00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:36.610
Like in App Engine, you
have a scaling boundary

00:27:36.610 --> 00:27:38.380
at your request deadline.

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:41.590
It can't go for more
than so many seconds,

00:27:41.590 --> 00:27:43.630
and it can't use more
than so much memory,

00:27:43.630 --> 00:27:45.910
depending on the type
of request it is.

00:27:45.910 --> 00:27:48.490
So there's a bunch of
different scaling boundaries

00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:52.390
that you might have to
anticipate and think about,

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:56.860
but the answer in all cases is
the same-- scale it outwards.

00:27:56.860 --> 00:27:59.740
So how do we do this?

00:27:59.740 --> 00:28:01.750
At Google, the term
we use is sharding,

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:04.420
and there's a lot of terms
across the industry for this.

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:07.000
And they all describe
the same technique,

00:28:07.000 --> 00:28:09.550
which is you take a
consistent workload,

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:12.610
and you divide it up
into little chunks that

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:14.560
can be done independently.

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:18.880
It's just divide and conquer
among a consistent workload.

00:28:18.880 --> 00:28:22.480
It's different from the noisy
neighbor scenario, which

00:28:22.480 --> 00:28:26.200
is more about different
workloads that were coincident

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:29.110
on the same resources.

00:28:29.110 --> 00:28:32.440
This is the same workload
that you can split up.

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:38.350
So if we take our
server, our API server,

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:41.170
and just replicate it three
times, then each one of those

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:44.320
can do 100,000 back
to the Vision API.

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:47.230
Then we can serve our
full 300,000 to our users,

00:28:47.230 --> 00:28:49.030
and everything is good.

00:28:49.030 --> 00:28:59.260
So we use more shards to
increase our capacity.

00:28:59.260 --> 00:29:01.030
So what do you use VM sizes for?

00:29:01.030 --> 00:29:02.560
Why do you even have that?

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:04.780
Well, those are really
good for getting best

00:29:04.780 --> 00:29:06.660
performance and efficiency.

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:09.130
So if your unit of
work lends itself

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:14.080
to having a quarter of a
terabyte of data in RAM,

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:17.930
then you want to pick a
machine that fits that size.

00:29:17.930 --> 00:29:21.910
But you don't want to just
have that unit of work

00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:25.390
be just growing over
time as your inputs grow

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:28.660
or something like that.

00:29:28.660 --> 00:29:31.470
And sharding is really hard.

00:29:31.470 --> 00:29:35.180
It's very difficult to
take your chunk of work,

00:29:35.180 --> 00:29:38.249
and figure out how to
make it independent

00:29:38.249 --> 00:29:39.290
across multiple machines.

00:29:39.290 --> 00:29:42.200
So start super early on this.

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:49.280
And think about it a lot, and
try little ideas and prototype

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:53.400
a lot to get the right balance.

00:29:53.400 --> 00:29:54.480
So it takes a while.

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:58.470
But best practice is as soon
as you find yourself wanting

00:29:58.470 --> 00:30:02.340
to crank up that instance type
and make it a little bigger,

00:30:02.340 --> 00:30:05.400
file the bug to
implement the sharding,

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:12.480
and keep it pretty
high on your backlog.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:14.700
So after we shard,
we're all super happy,

00:30:14.700 --> 00:30:17.160
because we know that every
single one of our instances

00:30:17.160 --> 00:30:20.280
is going to get 100,000 QPS,
but, of course, that never

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:21.180
happens.

00:30:21.180 --> 00:30:23.190
One of your shards is
going to get super busy.

00:30:23.190 --> 00:30:26.440
One of them is going to
have the celebrity on it,

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:29.430
or it's going to
represent New York

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:32.280
or Tokyo if you've sharded
across geography or something

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:32.980
like that.

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:35.920
So one of them is
going to get hot.

00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:38.640
So think about, how am I
going to balance that out

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:40.860
after it happens?

00:30:40.860 --> 00:30:43.050
You're going to need to reshard.

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:46.470
You can't just hope that they're
all going to be balanced out.

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:52.950
That's the SRE motto, actually,
is that hope is not a strategy.

00:30:52.950 --> 00:30:54.240
So how are we going reshard?

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:57.150
Well, one of the
things that you can do

00:30:57.150 --> 00:30:59.520
is implement a shard
map, so it's not

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:04.610
a static sharding from the data
to the idea of the node that's

00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:05.670
serving it.

00:31:05.670 --> 00:31:07.650
It's actually a lookup table.

00:31:07.650 --> 00:31:09.420
So you can put
something in the middle,

00:31:09.420 --> 00:31:13.110
and you can even use that
technique to get one hot key

00:31:13.110 --> 00:31:16.650
and put it somewhere else.

00:31:16.650 --> 00:31:21.390
It's more complex, because you
have multiple nodes this time.

00:31:21.390 --> 00:31:25.620
So it takes a little more work
than just pushing something

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:26.820
to a single node.

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:28.530
Like you might be
able to get away

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:32.857
with resharding a
single node at a time

00:31:32.857 --> 00:31:34.440
while leaving the
other ones together.

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:37.110
And that is what we
call splitting a shard.

00:31:37.110 --> 00:31:39.090
This is like the
primary technique that's

00:31:39.090 --> 00:31:41.670
like the go-to move if
your shard gets too big

00:31:41.670 --> 00:31:43.140
is just split it in half.

00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:44.579
This is how Bigtable works.

00:31:44.579 --> 00:31:46.370
It just waits until
the shard gets too big,

00:31:46.370 --> 00:31:49.500
and then it splits it in
half, and then does it again.

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:51.510
It's not super reliable.

00:31:51.510 --> 00:31:54.570
Like if you have one key on
the shard that's super hot,

00:31:54.570 --> 00:31:58.650
splitting it in half won't
really help that much.

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:03.747
But it works in a lot of cases,
so it's a good way to start.

00:32:03.747 --> 00:32:05.580
One of the nice things
about splitting shard

00:32:05.580 --> 00:32:07.595
is you can just do it
just with that one shard,

00:32:07.595 --> 00:32:09.720
and you don't have to worry
about the rest of them.

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:11.640
You can just keep
them running, and you

00:32:11.640 --> 00:32:15.150
don't have to like update your
global shard map or anything.

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:18.050
So I recommend it.

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:22.720
And if you have a super hot
key, probably resharding it

00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:23.970
is not going to actually help.

00:32:23.970 --> 00:32:26.430
You probably need to think
about moving that out and making

00:32:26.430 --> 00:32:28.920
it a separate workload
or separate subsystem.

00:32:28.920 --> 00:32:32.850
Like put it into cache, or
make a dedicated subsystem

00:32:32.850 --> 00:32:34.620
that can actually
serve that thing.

00:32:34.620 --> 00:32:37.980
If that thing itself
is 100,000 QPS,

00:32:37.980 --> 00:32:40.941
you might want some
special attention.

00:32:43.890 --> 00:32:46.710
The end of the pretty pictures--

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:51.690
so what we say in the cloud
is that we prefer cattle, not

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:52.400
pets.

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:56.610
And this is just how we think
about the subsystems that

00:32:56.610 --> 00:32:58.590
compose our service.

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:00.390
The classic one is a VM.

00:33:00.390 --> 00:33:04.730
So a pet is a nice
thing, and you probably

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:08.300
have some subsystems in your
service that have cute names,

00:33:08.300 --> 00:33:10.110
and everybody knows
what they are.

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:14.940
And people refer
to them by name.

00:33:14.940 --> 00:33:16.190
And if they die, then you cry.

00:33:16.190 --> 00:33:18.570
You're very sad.

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:20.900
But if cattle dies, it's
the cost of doing business.

00:33:20.900 --> 00:33:24.560
And that's kind of the
way we think about it.

00:33:24.560 --> 00:33:27.500
I was always really
impressed when I first

00:33:27.500 --> 00:33:32.570
got to Google with how little
tears were shed when a very

00:33:32.570 --> 00:33:34.910
important machine went down.

00:33:34.910 --> 00:33:38.090
There was just a
script that you ran.

00:33:38.090 --> 00:33:41.310
There was just like
throw away the machine.

00:33:41.310 --> 00:33:43.850
They would just take the
machine out of all the configs,

00:33:43.850 --> 00:33:46.310
and send somebody
in the data center

00:33:46.310 --> 00:33:50.900
to go physically fuss with it.

00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:53.840
And it made a lot of sense once
you got your head around it

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:55.610
that as soon as you
detect that there's

00:33:55.610 --> 00:33:57.485
something wrong with
it, don't try to fix it.

00:33:57.485 --> 00:34:02.090
Just get rid of it, because
you have another technique

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:07.460
to actually auto-heal or bring
another one back into the herd.

00:34:07.460 --> 00:34:10.440
So pets are often one
of the hardest things

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:16.130
to deal with because of the
human factors around them.

00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:20.060
A lot of pets have
like a handler,

00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:22.340
and you probably know some
of these in your systems,

00:34:22.340 --> 00:34:24.368
where there's a certain
person, and they're

00:34:24.368 --> 00:34:26.659
the only person that knows
how to deal with this thing.

00:34:26.659 --> 00:34:29.130
And it gets very upset if
anyone else touches it.

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:32.360
And so we leave it alone
until that person comes back

00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:34.229
in the office, and
everybody is very scared,

00:34:34.229 --> 00:34:36.770
even though they realize it's
absolutely critical for running

00:34:36.770 --> 00:34:37.556
the service.

00:34:37.556 --> 00:34:39.889
So if you see that kind of
thing happening, where people

00:34:39.889 --> 00:34:41.989
are scared to touch
a machine, that's

00:34:41.989 --> 00:34:44.460
a good sign that it's a pet.

00:34:44.460 --> 00:34:47.730
And also, when you
see people trying

00:34:47.730 --> 00:34:50.639
to be heroic around their
pets and be the fireman who

00:34:50.639 --> 00:34:55.977
goes and saves the cat from
being stuck in the tree,

00:34:55.977 --> 00:34:57.810
that's a good sign that
you have a pet also.

00:34:57.810 --> 00:35:02.040
You should just let the cat get
down from the tree by itself,

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:02.809
if it needs to.

00:35:02.809 --> 00:35:03.600
There's other cats.

00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:13.230
So the best tools I know
for getting rid of pets

00:35:13.230 --> 00:35:17.910
are just acknowledging
that there's a pet

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:19.440
and documenting it.

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:21.990
And once you've written it down
that this thing is critical,

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:24.690
and nobody knows how to deal
with it, and when it breaks,

00:35:24.690 --> 00:35:28.650
our whole service goes
down, it starts to sink in.

00:35:28.650 --> 00:35:32.390
And people get over their fear
and start working on solutions.

00:35:32.390 --> 00:35:34.140
So I definitely recommend
writing it down.

00:35:38.680 --> 00:35:42.530
You might have experienced this.

00:35:42.530 --> 00:35:45.700
Outages often occur when
you change something.

00:35:45.700 --> 00:35:49.716
So don't change things, right?

00:35:49.716 --> 00:35:52.081
[LAUGHS]

00:35:53.030 --> 00:35:57.300
This is the number
one cause of outages

00:35:57.300 --> 00:35:59.610
in every system I've
ever worked with is

00:35:59.610 --> 00:36:01.612
people deploying something.

00:36:01.612 --> 00:36:05.600
At Google, we think of
the config and the code

00:36:05.600 --> 00:36:07.460
in our system as really
one and the same.

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:10.970
We don't really treat
them any differently

00:36:10.970 --> 00:36:15.320
in terms of how we manage them
in the source code repository.

00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:18.700
We put all the config in
the source code repository,

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:24.800
and we do rollouts of config
and code in very similar ways.

00:36:24.800 --> 00:36:26.567
It's kind of like
config is just like code

00:36:26.567 --> 00:36:28.400
that can be pushed up
with greater frequency

00:36:28.400 --> 00:36:30.750
or something like that.

00:36:30.750 --> 00:36:33.920
And we treat config like
code by unit testing it

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:38.560
and sanity checking it, just
like you would with code.

00:36:38.560 --> 00:36:44.440
So the best techniques
for not deploying bad code

00:36:44.440 --> 00:36:48.640
are to have a good way of
recovering when you do,

00:36:48.640 --> 00:36:53.410
because it is just
so inevitable.

00:36:53.410 --> 00:36:55.990
A lot of the time,
people will try

00:36:55.990 --> 00:36:59.230
to fix something hot after
they've pushed some bad code.

00:36:59.230 --> 00:37:04.060
They'll try to
debug it and fix it.

00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:06.070
And the absolutely most
important thing to do

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:08.491
is have a tried and
true rollback process,

00:37:08.491 --> 00:37:10.240
where you just have a
big red Easy button,

00:37:10.240 --> 00:37:13.570
and you can just smash
it as soon as you

00:37:13.570 --> 00:37:15.820
notice anything going
wrong, while a deployment

00:37:15.820 --> 00:37:16.980
is happening.

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:19.060
So precondition for
that is you have

00:37:19.060 --> 00:37:22.450
to know when a
deployment is happening.

00:37:22.450 --> 00:37:24.490
At Google, we have
a database where

00:37:24.490 --> 00:37:26.380
everything that
deploys something

00:37:26.380 --> 00:37:28.070
posts a record
into this database.

00:37:28.070 --> 00:37:30.760
And so you can kind of
go and look it up and see

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:35.670
if some component is changing
that's related to the alert

00:37:35.670 --> 00:37:37.234
you're getting.

00:37:37.234 --> 00:37:39.400
And you probably want to
do something like that too.

00:37:39.400 --> 00:37:43.060
People do stuff as simple
as like a Slackbot that

00:37:43.060 --> 00:37:45.640
just records every
deployment that's

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:48.879
happening to using
something that's

00:37:48.879 --> 00:37:50.420
pretty advanced like
Spinnaker, where

00:37:50.420 --> 00:37:53.380
you can actually see what's
going on and control it.

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:58.660
But the most important thing
is when Operator A on System A

00:37:58.660 --> 00:38:00.760
is noticing that
something is going wrong,

00:38:00.760 --> 00:38:03.460
they look at A's
53 dependencies.

00:38:03.460 --> 00:38:07.990
They can see which ones
have changed in the past 15

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:09.410
minutes or something.

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:13.180
And it allows you to then
hit that Easy Rollback

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:15.580
button on the right system.

00:38:15.580 --> 00:38:18.690
It's not bad to hit a rollback.

00:38:18.690 --> 00:38:22.540
We often roll things back,
and then roll them forward

00:38:22.540 --> 00:38:25.870
again not much later,
because the rollback--

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:27.240
it was like a false alarm.

00:38:27.240 --> 00:38:28.690
You roll it back.

00:38:28.690 --> 00:38:32.110
You end up kind of delaying the
progress of the whole rollout

00:38:32.110 --> 00:38:34.570
by, I don't know, a
couple hours or something.

00:38:34.570 --> 00:38:36.400
But it's a really
good peace of mind.

00:38:36.400 --> 00:38:39.760
It's really a good thing just to
get in the habit of like muscle

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:42.120
memory of just doing it.

00:38:42.120 --> 00:38:44.061
So once you have
that, and you're

00:38:44.061 --> 00:38:45.810
really confident that
your rollback works,

00:38:45.810 --> 00:38:48.360
and you just hit
it every time, you

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:51.630
can start to automate your
rollbacks by having something

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:56.640
like a canary watcher that
watches the first machine that

00:38:56.640 --> 00:38:59.580
gets your new code and sees
if it goes error condition.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:02.562
If it goes error,
it just stops the--

00:39:02.562 --> 00:39:04.200
just stop and roll back.

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:05.894
Take that machine.

00:39:05.894 --> 00:39:07.560
Take it out of the
service, and then you

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:09.860
can do forensics on it,
[? whatever, ?] on the side.

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:13.630
I said canary.

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:16.320
That will probably give the
idea that progressive rollouts

00:39:16.320 --> 00:39:17.559
are really a good idea.

00:39:17.559 --> 00:39:19.600
So you think about just
sending it to one machine

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:23.440
first, and then maybe 10
machines, and then maybe

00:39:23.440 --> 00:39:27.132
a whole zone, and then
maybe a whole region.

00:39:27.132 --> 00:39:28.840
And the rule of thumb
that you want to do

00:39:28.840 --> 00:39:31.060
is you want to go slowly
enough that you can detect

00:39:31.060 --> 00:39:33.421
the issues that will emerge.

00:39:33.421 --> 00:39:35.170
So you don't want to
just kind of like run

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:38.290
through those stages
automatically.

00:39:38.290 --> 00:39:40.330
You might want to go a
little faster from one

00:39:40.330 --> 00:39:45.670
machine to 10 machines than
you do from zone to region,

00:39:45.670 --> 00:39:48.670
because you have to give
yourself time to figure it out.

00:39:48.670 --> 00:39:51.340
For example, one thing
that people often

00:39:51.340 --> 00:39:56.860
do with the global system is
they roll it out globally too

00:39:56.860 --> 00:39:57.530
quickly.

00:39:57.530 --> 00:40:00.070
Like if you roll
down into a region,

00:40:00.070 --> 00:40:04.450
and it happens to be
sleepytime in that region,

00:40:04.450 --> 00:40:07.330
and you don't notice, because
all the users are asleep.

00:40:07.330 --> 00:40:08.890
And then you go ahead, and
you roll it out globally,

00:40:08.890 --> 00:40:09.970
and then the users
wake up, and then

00:40:09.970 --> 00:40:12.095
you realize you have a
global outage on your hands.

00:40:12.095 --> 00:40:12.940
So just go slowly.

00:40:12.940 --> 00:40:18.100
Make sure you have a good
bake-in time for each stage

00:40:18.100 --> 00:40:20.430
of your progressive rollout.

00:40:20.430 --> 00:40:22.550
But if you need a rule of
thumb, a pretty good one

00:40:22.550 --> 00:40:31.160
is to do like a 1%, 5%, 10%,
50%, 100% kind of strategy

00:40:31.160 --> 00:40:32.760
to ramp up.

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:36.680
That gives you
enough easy ramp time

00:40:36.680 --> 00:40:41.780
in that you'll probably
catch most of the problems

00:40:41.780 --> 00:40:43.900
on the first machine.

00:40:43.900 --> 00:40:48.140
You often don't know if
it's just the machine itself

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:50.750
or if it's the new
code, but by the time

00:40:50.750 --> 00:40:53.240
you get to 50% or 100%, you
can actually go pretty fast,

00:40:53.240 --> 00:40:56.594
because you've already
done a few increases.

00:40:59.380 --> 00:41:02.309
The main problem
people have with doing

00:41:02.309 --> 00:41:04.600
progressive rollouts, though,
is that they don't really

00:41:04.600 --> 00:41:08.980
understand whether the
new software is actually

00:41:08.980 --> 00:41:10.792
working correctly or not.

00:41:10.792 --> 00:41:12.250
And this is because
a lot of people

00:41:12.250 --> 00:41:15.700
have monitoring gaps in
their system between what

00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:18.760
the users' expectation is and
what the monitoring is showing.

00:41:18.760 --> 00:41:23.380
So it's very typical that people
will be monitoring CPU and RAM

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:26.650
usage on a server, put
some new code on it,

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:30.220
and then they see the CPU
and RAM usage change a lot.

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:32.452
And then they kind of wonder
if that's a big problem.

00:41:32.452 --> 00:41:33.910
And then some time
passes, and they

00:41:33.910 --> 00:41:35.860
decide it's not a big problem.

00:41:35.860 --> 00:41:38.980
And then they just kind
of let the rollout go on.

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:40.990
And maybe it is a big problem.

00:41:40.990 --> 00:41:42.440
Maybe it's not a big problem.

00:41:42.440 --> 00:41:44.200
We don't really know.

00:41:44.200 --> 00:41:48.040
But the real test is, did your
users think it's a problem?

00:41:48.040 --> 00:41:53.200
So the ideal scenario is if
you can have a monitoring

00:41:53.200 --> 00:41:55.570
metric that actually
predicts your users'

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:58.630
opinion of your
service, so it's not

00:41:58.630 --> 00:42:01.950
that hard to think about how
many errors are you serving.

00:42:01.950 --> 00:42:05.020
Was there a big change in
latency that you're serving?

00:42:05.020 --> 00:42:07.390
A lot of times, you'll
add new features

00:42:07.390 --> 00:42:08.560
to your service over time.

00:42:08.560 --> 00:42:11.140
And your CPU usage
will go up quite a bit

00:42:11.140 --> 00:42:14.490
when you put out a new
feature, or it's very common

00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:16.240
that a new feature
will have a new dataset

00:42:16.240 --> 00:42:17.380
that it needs to pull in.

00:42:17.380 --> 00:42:19.940
So you push out
your new feature.

00:42:19.940 --> 00:42:22.450
It does a whole bunch of
I/O and a whole bunch of CPU

00:42:22.450 --> 00:42:24.670
to pull all the
data in into RAM,

00:42:24.670 --> 00:42:28.160
and put it into 40 different
tables so it can serve.

00:42:28.160 --> 00:42:29.860
It looks really scary
to the operator,

00:42:29.860 --> 00:42:31.443
because you just put
some new code in,

00:42:31.443 --> 00:42:34.420
and it does all this stuff,
and you don't understand.

00:42:34.420 --> 00:42:36.700
But the most important
thing is, did you

00:42:36.700 --> 00:42:39.730
think about exporting a
metric, about the success

00:42:39.730 --> 00:42:41.470
of that new feature?

00:42:41.470 --> 00:42:46.300
So you can see if that metric
is trending like you like.

00:42:46.300 --> 00:42:50.110
So then if you detect that
the users are unhappy,

00:42:50.110 --> 00:42:53.050
that's when you dive down
to your CPU and network

00:42:53.050 --> 00:42:55.945
and RAM stuff just
for diagnostics.

00:42:55.945 --> 00:43:00.340
It'll try to give you a clue
as to what's going wrong.

00:43:00.340 --> 00:43:03.250
The most useful
thing you can do is

00:43:03.250 --> 00:43:05.590
build these types
of metrics right

00:43:05.590 --> 00:43:07.930
into your microservices
libraries.

00:43:07.930 --> 00:43:12.100
So in Google, we
have an RPC layer

00:43:12.100 --> 00:43:15.940
called Stubby, which gives you
a client stub and a server stub.

00:43:15.940 --> 00:43:19.580
And it's great, because
the client stub exports

00:43:19.580 --> 00:43:22.810
how many requests it's
making to the server,

00:43:22.810 --> 00:43:25.750
and the server exports how
many requests it's getting.

00:43:25.750 --> 00:43:27.790
And they have
consistent definition,

00:43:27.790 --> 00:43:31.027
and they operate on
similar pipelines.

00:43:31.027 --> 00:43:32.360
So they arrive at the same time.

00:43:32.360 --> 00:43:34.818
And so you can just make a
graph of how many did the client

00:43:34.818 --> 00:43:36.580
send, how many did
the server get,

00:43:36.580 --> 00:43:40.660
and it's like dead easy to tell
if there's a network problem.

00:43:40.660 --> 00:43:44.170
So a lot of services
I see being built,

00:43:44.170 --> 00:43:46.280
people kind of try to
monitor on the side,

00:43:46.280 --> 00:43:48.280
or they try to monitor
off of logs or something.

00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:50.290
But it's really,
really, really helpful

00:43:50.290 --> 00:43:54.760
to build it into your network
client and server libraries

00:43:54.760 --> 00:43:57.729
to export these high
level metrics right

00:43:57.729 --> 00:43:59.770
to your monitoring system,
so you can really tell

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:00.895
if something's going wrong.

00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:06.660
This is the last one.

00:44:06.660 --> 00:44:09.440
You made it.

00:44:09.440 --> 00:44:14.480
You really need to understand
what are your failure domains.

00:44:14.480 --> 00:44:17.120
These are chunks of
your infrastructure

00:44:17.120 --> 00:44:19.970
that can fail all
together at the same time.

00:44:19.970 --> 00:44:26.300
So obviously, a VM can fail,
and all the processes on

00:44:26.300 --> 00:44:30.230
that VM are gone, clearly.

00:44:30.230 --> 00:44:32.790
And obviously, a
zone and a region

00:44:32.790 --> 00:44:34.490
have-- there are
also failure domains

00:44:34.490 --> 00:44:40.382
that we talk about explicitly,
don't even want to think about.

00:44:40.382 --> 00:44:42.090
If you know your
failure domain, then you

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:45.630
can do really smart things
like put your backup

00:44:45.630 --> 00:44:49.210
in another failure domain,
which a lot of people

00:44:49.210 --> 00:44:52.922
kind of don't do
for some reason.

00:44:52.922 --> 00:44:54.880
But it's really important
to be thinking about,

00:44:54.880 --> 00:44:56.740
where am I going
to actually recover

00:44:56.740 --> 00:44:59.970
from when I need to recover?

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:03.000
A lot of outages I've
seen get to a point,

00:45:03.000 --> 00:45:04.740
where people are
thinking of, oh,

00:45:04.740 --> 00:45:09.120
should I stand up a new
cluster in a new zone,

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:10.672
because this one's down?

00:45:10.672 --> 00:45:12.130
And then they start
thinking, well,

00:45:12.130 --> 00:45:13.590
which zone should I choose?

00:45:13.590 --> 00:45:16.820
This is a decision you
can make way beforehand,

00:45:16.820 --> 00:45:19.800
and you can choose what's your
new failure domain going to be,

00:45:19.800 --> 00:45:22.440
and where you're
going to recover

00:45:22.440 --> 00:45:24.172
it to like way ahead of time.

00:45:24.172 --> 00:45:26.505
And put your backups there,
and put all your data there,

00:45:26.505 --> 00:45:27.910
so you're ready to go.

00:45:27.910 --> 00:45:29.610
You don't have to
make any decisions.

00:45:29.610 --> 00:45:31.560
Making decisions when
your system is down

00:45:31.560 --> 00:45:34.650
is the worst possible
scenario, because you're all

00:45:34.650 --> 00:45:36.660
full of cortisol,
and you're crazy.

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:38.340
So just try to make
as many decisions

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:40.614
as you can ahead of time.

00:45:40.614 --> 00:45:42.280
And you want to use
your failure domains

00:45:42.280 --> 00:45:45.800
for a lot of your
day-to-day operations.

00:45:45.800 --> 00:45:48.840
Like make sure that
your failure domains are

00:45:48.840 --> 00:45:52.170
in your monitoring
as a dimension

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:55.140
that you can cut by,
because if a zone goes down,

00:45:55.140 --> 00:45:58.230
then you want to see that zone
went down, not just like nodes

00:45:58.230 --> 00:46:01.750
one through 49 went down.

00:46:01.750 --> 00:46:04.210
You want to use it,
also, in your rollouts.

00:46:04.210 --> 00:46:06.410
So you want to roll out
to this zone, and then

00:46:06.410 --> 00:46:10.890
that zone, and then that zone,
because if the zone goes down

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:13.260
in the middle of
your rollout, you

00:46:13.260 --> 00:46:14.970
want to be able to
be sure that it's

00:46:14.970 --> 00:46:18.250
the zone going down and not
your rollout taking it down.

00:46:18.250 --> 00:46:21.210
If you imagine if you rolled
out to multiple failure domains

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:24.000
at the same time, then it would
be a little harder to tell

00:46:24.000 --> 00:46:25.520
what's going on.

00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:28.380
And you want to
actually disaster test

00:46:28.380 --> 00:46:30.930
on the edges of those
failure domains.

00:46:30.930 --> 00:46:37.770
So we like doing destructive
disaster testing at Google,

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:40.530
and we'll just take
a node and shoot it.

00:46:40.530 --> 00:46:43.340
And just like the
Whack-a-pod kind of thing.

00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:46.080
Or we'll take a whole zone
and just knock it out,

00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:49.820
and see what the operators do,
see how the system recovers.

00:46:49.820 --> 00:46:52.980
So it's a good boundary
to think about.

00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:55.800
And you can also
make failure domains

00:46:55.800 --> 00:46:58.710
within your application
like synthetic ones.

00:46:58.710 --> 00:47:00.899
So you could make--

00:47:00.899 --> 00:47:01.440
I don't know.

00:47:01.440 --> 00:47:05.430
You could have your power
users be in one logical failure

00:47:05.430 --> 00:47:07.680
domain, and then everybody
who signed up yesterday

00:47:07.680 --> 00:47:09.840
be in another one
so that you can

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:14.220
think about what would happen
if we sacrificed those people.

00:47:14.220 --> 00:47:18.750
Would we still be getting a
significant amount of traffic

00:47:18.750 --> 00:47:20.234
from the rest of them or not?

00:47:20.234 --> 00:47:21.150
And what would happen?

00:47:21.150 --> 00:47:27.690
So you can get kind of
creative about how you fail.

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:28.350
All right.

00:47:28.350 --> 00:47:29.030
We made it.

00:47:29.030 --> 00:47:30.810
So we know all that stuff.

00:47:30.810 --> 00:47:35.070
A lot of it is not super
rocket science, and a lot of it

00:47:35.070 --> 00:47:39.360
is stuff you've probably
seen before and heard before.

00:47:39.360 --> 00:47:43.480
But it really is important,
and it bears repeating.

00:47:43.480 --> 00:47:47.280
So how do we actually
get the nines?

00:47:47.280 --> 00:47:50.310
Just knowing about the
stuff is not worth it.

00:47:50.310 --> 00:47:52.020
It doesn't give you
automatically nines.

00:47:52.020 --> 00:47:55.110
You have to act on it.

00:47:55.110 --> 00:47:58.950
There are hundreds of bugs
out there, which are just

00:47:58.950 --> 00:48:02.220
little time bombs waiting to
go off, because someone knows

00:48:02.220 --> 00:48:06.600
that there's a problem,
and they can't really

00:48:06.600 --> 00:48:09.450
justify putting in all the
work to fix it right now.

00:48:09.450 --> 00:48:12.720
So a good technique
to think about that

00:48:12.720 --> 00:48:17.070
is think about the criticality
and the impact of a problem

00:48:17.070 --> 00:48:19.080
and how likely it is to occur.

00:48:19.080 --> 00:48:21.260
So is it a critical
situation that's

00:48:21.260 --> 00:48:23.010
going to take your
service completely down

00:48:23.010 --> 00:48:24.900
like if your pet dies?

00:48:24.900 --> 00:48:28.110
Or is it something that's going
to maybe degrade your service,

00:48:28.110 --> 00:48:29.760
or maybe you won't
even notice at all?

00:48:29.760 --> 00:48:32.510
And then how frequently
is it likely to happen?

00:48:32.510 --> 00:48:34.740
Is it going to
happen multiple times

00:48:34.740 --> 00:48:37.590
during a single
on-call shift, or is it

00:48:37.590 --> 00:48:40.215
going to happen maybe once
a month or once a year?

00:48:40.215 --> 00:48:44.460
And then you can think about
quantifying how much time it's

00:48:44.460 --> 00:48:46.170
worth to actually
invest in these things,

00:48:46.170 --> 00:48:49.230
and then scheduling out the time
over several weeks or months

00:48:49.230 --> 00:48:52.830
to actually get some of
these bigger chunks done.

00:48:52.830 --> 00:48:55.020
There's been far
too many outages

00:48:55.020 --> 00:48:58.182
that just went like, oh, yeah,
I knew that was a problem.

00:48:58.182 --> 00:48:59.640
We've known about
it for two years,

00:48:59.640 --> 00:49:03.230
but we just never
bothered to fix it.

00:49:03.230 --> 00:49:07.320
And that really comes down
to just analyzing stuff.

00:49:07.320 --> 00:49:12.390
I'd like to remind the
developers that reliability

00:49:12.390 --> 00:49:15.130
is the number one feature
of your application

00:49:15.130 --> 00:49:17.040
is that it works.

00:49:17.040 --> 00:49:21.450
And so it's completely
legit to file a bug

00:49:21.450 --> 00:49:23.280
and say there's a
reliability problem,

00:49:23.280 --> 00:49:26.520
and have a discussion
with your product managers

00:49:26.520 --> 00:49:32.220
about, where do we actually
put this in the features?

00:49:32.220 --> 00:49:33.630
Do we put it at the top?

00:49:33.630 --> 00:49:35.750
Usually, it comes
pretty much at the top.

00:49:35.750 --> 00:49:38.580
I've found most product managers
say, oh, yeah, of course.

00:49:38.580 --> 00:49:39.120
Obviously.

00:49:39.120 --> 00:49:41.480
I thought you were
doing that all the time.

00:49:41.480 --> 00:49:45.300
But it's good to have that
clear conversation about it

00:49:45.300 --> 00:49:48.230
to make sure that you
actually get to it in time.

00:49:48.230 --> 00:49:51.140
And so these are
things we can actually

00:49:51.140 --> 00:49:53.810
anticipate and prevent,
because they're fairly generic,

00:49:53.810 --> 00:49:56.330
and they'll pretty much
happen to everybody.

00:49:56.330 --> 00:49:58.790
But, of course, we can't
anticipate everything.

00:49:58.790 --> 00:50:04.740
So we're going to fall back
on a lot of our SRE principles

00:50:04.740 --> 00:50:07.320
and practices to deal
with all those things

00:50:07.320 --> 00:50:10.440
like do a blameless post-mortem.

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:12.720
Do your disaster testing.

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:16.830
Do your reliability
reviews by actually-- what

00:50:16.830 --> 00:50:20.370
I mean by that is
get the design ready.

00:50:20.370 --> 00:50:23.100
Talk about the SLOs and
what are the requirements.

00:50:23.100 --> 00:50:27.450
Get a bunch of disinterested
engineers, colleagues

00:50:27.450 --> 00:50:30.630
or friends, in the room to
try to poke holes in it.

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:32.777
And make that as
part of a practice.

00:50:32.777 --> 00:50:35.110
And that's the way to get
those incremental improvements

00:50:35.110 --> 00:50:38.310
for these weird things that
you can't really anticipate.

00:50:38.310 --> 00:50:41.280
That's going to be the thing
that's going to take us forward

00:50:41.280 --> 00:50:45.141
to three nines and beyond.

00:50:45.141 --> 00:50:45.640
All right.

00:50:45.640 --> 00:50:47.350
Thanks for your time, everybody.

00:50:47.350 --> 00:50:51.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

